+## Mutating statements
+
+`UPDATE` and `DELETE` read one base table, so they skip the plan entirely:
+`planner::dml` binds the target's schema into a scope, resolves each `SET`
+column to a position, and hands `api` a predicate plus assignments. `api` builds
+and validates every candidate row against the table before writing a single
+column, which is what makes a statement all-or-nothing, and it reads assignment
+right-hand sides from the stored row rather than from the row it is building, so
+assignments cannot see each other. Storage then either overwrites rows in place
+or compacts each column vector around the removed positions, leaving the
+survivors in their stored order.
+
